"Coloring one-headed directed hypergraphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.00189)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Title: Coloring one-headed directed hypergraphs ... The main result of this paper is that the conjecture is true for one-headed directed hypergraphs with all hyperedges having size at least three.\nTitle: Coloring one-headed directed hypergraphs\nAuthors: Balázs István Szabó\nDate: Fri Feb 28 21:11:46 2025\n\nA directed hypergraph is a hypergraph in which the vertex set of each hyperedge is partitioned into two disjoint parts, a head and a tail. Keszegh and Pálvölgyi posed the following conjecture. Let $H$ be a directed hypergraph such that in every hyperedge the number of head-vertices is less than the number of tail-vertices and assume that for every pair of hyperedges $e_{1},e_{2}\\in E(H)$ with $|e_{1}\\cap e_{2}|=1$, the common vertex is a head-vertex in at least one of the hyperedges. Then $H$ admits a proper 2-coloring. Keszegh showed that the conjecture is also true in the special case of 3-uniform hypergraphs. A directed hypergraph is called one-headed if every hyperedge has exactly one head-vertex. The main result of this paper is that the conjecture is true for one-headed directed hypergraphs with all hyperedges having size at least three. Directed 3-uniform hypergraphs such that in every hyperedge the number of head-vertices is one and the number of tail-vertices is two are called $2\\rightarrow 1$ hypergraphs. In this paper we consider sufficient conditions for $2\\rightarrow 1$ hypergraphs to be proper $k$-colorable for some small $k$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nColoring one-headed directed hypergraphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2503.00189v1)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Coloring one-headed directed hypergraphs ... The main result of this paper is that the conjecture is true for one-headed directed hypergraphs with all hyperedges having size at least three.\n\n## Coloring one-headed directed hypergraphs\n\nBalázs István Szabó\n\nPublished: 2025-02-28\n\n\ndirected hypergraph proper coloring coloring\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nA directed hypergraph is a hypergraph in which the vertex set of each hyperedge is partitioned into two disjoint parts, a head and a tail. Keszegh and Pálvölgyi posed the following conjecture. Let $H$ be a directed hypergraph such that in every hyperedge the number of head-vertices is less than the number of tail-vertices and assume that for every pair of hyperedges $e_{1},e_{2}\\in E(H)$ with $|e_{1}\\cap e_{2}|=1$, the common vertex is a head-vertex in at least one of the hyperedges. Then $H$ admits a proper 2-coloring. Keszegh showed that the conjecture is also true in the special case of 3-uniform hypergraphs. A directed hypergraph is called one-headed if every hyperedge has exactly one head-vertex. The main result of this paper is that the conjecture is true for one-headed directed hypergraphs with all hyperedges having size at least three. Directed 3-uniform hypergraphs such that in every hyperedge the number of head-vertices is one and the number of tail-vertices is two are called $2\\rightarrow 1$ hypergraphs. In this paper we consider sufficient conditions for $2\\rightarrow 1$ hypergraphs to be proper $k$-colorable for some small $k$.\n\n### BibTeX\n\n[Button: arXiv BibTeX ] [Button: Published BibTeX ]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n\nMaintained by Per Alexandersson, per@symmetricfunctions.com\n\nBibTeX [Button: ×]\n\n[Button: Copy all]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPetrov's balls conjecture for biregular labeled multigraphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/373705/petrov-s-balls-conjecture-for-biregular-labeled-multigraphs)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Petrov's balls conjecture for biregular labeled multigraphs ... In the balls-and-colors interpretation, this asserts that the balls can be paired or left fixed so that every girl ends with exactly one ball of each color, with every ball involved in at most one swap. ... Bogdanov, Fedor Petrov, Anton Sadovnichiy and Fedor Ushakov, “Biregular bipartite labeled multigraphs and perfect matchings in bipartite tensor products”, arXiv:2603.18253 (2026).\n\n# \n\nPetrov's balls conjecture for biregular labeled multigraphs\n\nLess than 1 year old · traced to 2026\n\nLet a bipartite labeled multigraph be a tuple $(L,R,E,u,v)$(L,R,E,u,v), where $u:E\\to L$u:E→L and $v:E\\to R$v:E→R. Let $A_{n,k}$A n,k​ be the set of biregular bipartite labeled multigraphs with $L=[n]$L=[n], $R=[k]$R=[k], and $E=[n]\\times[k]$E=[n]×[k]; let $I$I be the set of involutions in the permutation group of $[n]\\times[k]$[n]×[k]. For functions $u,v$u,v with common domain $E$E, let $[u,v]$[u,v] denote the weighted $L\\times R$L×R matrix whose $(x,y)$(x,y) entry counts edges $e$e with $u(e)=x$u(e)=x and $v(e)=y$v(e)=y, and let $W_{[n],[k]}$W[n],[k]​ be the all-ones matrix. Petrov's balls conjecture. For every $(u,v)\\in A_{n,k}$(u,v)∈A n,k​, there exists an involution $\\iota\\in I$ι∈I such that\n\n$$[u\\circ\\iota,v]=W_{[n],[k]}.$$[u∘ι,v]=W[n],[k]​.\n\nIn the balls-and-colors interpretation, this asserts that the balls can be paired or left fixed so that every girl ends with exactly one ball of each color, with every ball involved in at most one swap. The conjecture implies the weak balls conjecture, but the general statement remains unproved; special cases are established in the paper.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nIlya I. Bogdanov, Fedor Petrov, Anton Sadovnichiy and Fedor Ushakov, “Biregular bipartite labeled multigraphs and perfect matchings in bipartite tensor products”, arXiv:2603.18253 (2026).\n\nbipartite-graphscombinatoricsperfect-matchings\n\n0 0\n\n4 weeks ago\n\n## Progres