Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026 ... We prove a uniform form of the dynamical Manin-Mumford conjecture for split endomorphisms $Φ= \\left(f_1,\\ldots,f_n\\right): \\left(\\mathbb{P}^1\\right)^n \\to \\left(\\mathbb{P}^1\\right)^n$ over $\\mathbb{C}$, where each $f_i$ has degree $d \\geq 2$ and at most one $f_i$ is exceptional. ... Our approach combines a degeneration argument via ultrafilters with a new non-archimedean rigidity theorem, along with the quantitative dynamical Zhang's inequality by Mavraki and the second author.\nA p-adic Valuation Calculus for Permutations with Restricted Cycle Lengths\n\nAhaan Kallat\n\narXiv:2610.40342\n\nAuthors: Ahaan Kallat\n\nSubmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nLet a_A(n) be the number of permutations of {1,...,n} all of whose cycle lengths lie in a set A. We develop a valuation calculus for these numbers. Mobius inversion writes the exponential generating function as an Euler product, and at a prime p the factors with non-p-integral exponents, the blocks, determine a finite knapsack problem. Its optimal value gives a lower bound for v_p(a_A(n)), and the bound is attained exactly when one residue modulo p, the sum of the terms of the optimal choices, does not vanish. For a fixed set A and prime p the bounds and residues for all n <= N cost O(N^2) operations. When one block is strictly the most efficient, the valuation inside each residue class is that of a locally analytic p-adic function. We use the calculus to determine the 2-adic and 3-adic valuations for cycle lengths at most 3, 4 and 5, to prove the divisibility conjectures recorded in A378495, A079128, A193437 and A193438 and the 3-adic statements recorded in A057693 and A070945, and to treat 99 OEIS sequences in all. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRandom Structures & Algorithms 2025 (https://dmg.tuwien.ac.at/rsa2025/)\nciteturn1search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # Teodor Todorov Petrov ... We prove this conject